WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Suppose a Fourier computation becomes twice as fast.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Does the whole system become twice as fast? Pause and predict.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.000
Imagine sequential work taking one hundred milliseconds.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Fourier computation takes twenty. Everything else takes eighty.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.000
Halve the Fourier time: twenty milliseconds becomes ten.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.000
The other eighty milliseconds stays unchanged.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Ten plus eighty is ninety. We saved ten milliseconds.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Overall speedup is old time divided by new time.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.000
One hundred over ninety is about one point one one, not two.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.500
Even zero Fourier time leaves eighty milliseconds.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:49.000
In this model, the speedup limit is one point two five.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:53.000
Arithmetic complexity asks how operation counts grow.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.000
Numerical stability asks how computation errors behave.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.000
Latency measures elapsed time. Readiness needs a validated system.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.500
Check accuracy, data movement, and worst-case deadlines.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:10.000
This toy arithmetic proves no new FFT or real-time grid capability.

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Explore the fraction improved and its speedup in our Marimo lesson.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Fourier methods connect algorithms to latency—not automatically to readiness.
